   "section": "8.1",
   "bookAnswer": "",
   "keyReadsAgree": false,
   "indep": "A mathematical statement is a declarative sentence that may be either true or false but not both; a mathematical proof is a logical argument that demonstrates the truth of a mathematical statement. Examples of statements: 22/7 ∉ Q′; Q ⊆ R (true); 3 + 4 = 8; Z ⊆ W (false). Examples of mathematical proof: If x is an odd integer, then x² is also an odd integer; the sum of two odd numbers is an even number.",
   "status": "confirmed",
   "note": ""
  },
  {
   "source": "Ex 8 Q5",
   "section": "8.1",
   "bookAnswer": "",
   "keyReadsAgree": false,
   "indep": "A theorem is a mathematical statement proved true based on previously known facts; an axiom is a mathematical statement believed to be true without evidence or proof. Examples: the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees (theorem); through a given point, infinitely many lines can pass (axiom).",
   "status": "confirmed",
   "note": ""
  },
